// yours: scaffolded once, never touched again — forge will not overwrite this file
//
// The real {@link AuthProvider}: OIDC authorization-code flow with PKCE,
// against ANY compliant identity provider.
//
// This is the sibling of `session-provider.ts` (the mock, which is still
// there and still the right choice for pure-UI development). Which one your
// app runs on is decided by CONFIGURATION, in `selectAuthProvider()` at the
// bottom of this file — not by an environment flag that says "pretend".
//
// ── How the flow works, and where forge is not involved ───────────────
//
// forge's backend is validation-only: it checks bearer tokens and never mints
// them. Token acquisition is a conversation between this browser and your
// IdP that the forge server never sees, which is why there is no
// `/auth/login` endpoint anywhere in your project:
//
//   1. login()   — mint a PKCE verifier + state, stash them (see
//                  oidc-storage.ts), redirect to the IdP's /authorize.
//   2. the IdP   — authenticates the user itself, then redirects back to
//                  this app's callback with ?code= and ?state=.
//   3. callback  — verify state, take the verifier, POST both to the IdP's
//                  /token, hold the resulting token in memory.
//   4. every RPC — the Connect transport calls getToken() and attaches
//                  `Authorization: Bearer …`. The forge server validates it
//                  against the same issuer's JWKS.
//
// Step 4 is why `jwt_issuer` / `jwt_jwks_url` in your backend config must
// point at the SAME issuer configured here. That is the only coupling
// between the two halves.
//
// ── Configuring it: one issuer URL is enough ──────────────────────────
//
// Set these in your environment (they are inlined into the bundle at build
// time, and all three are PUBLIC values — a client id is an identifier, not
// a secret, and this is a public OAuth client by design):
//
{{- if eq .Platform "vite-spa"}}
//   VITE_OIDC_ISSUER       https://your-tenant.example.com/oidc
//   VITE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID    the client/application id from your IdP
//   VITE_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI optional; defaults to <origin>/auth/callback
//   VITE_OIDC_SCOPES       optional; defaults to "openid profile email offline_access"
{{- else}}
//   NEXT_PUBLIC_OIDC_ISSUER       https://your-tenant.example.com/oidc
//   NEXT_PUBLIC_OIDC_CLIENT_ID    the client/application id from your IdP
//   NEXT_PUBLIC_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI optional; defaults to <origin>/auth/callback
//   NEXT_PUBLIC_OIDC_SCOPES       optional; defaults to "openid profile email offline_access"
{{- end}}
//
// Everything else — the authorization endpoint, the token endpoint, the
// sign-out endpoint — is read from the issuer's OIDC discovery document, so
// nothing in this file names a provider. Auth0, Clerk, Keycloak, Zitadel and
// Okta all work by changing the issuer and client id, with no code change.
// Register `<origin>/auth/callback` as an allowed redirect URI with
// whichever one you use.

